x\nL379@P4: h√\nL380@P4: xUj\nL381@P4: ³\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.10030v1)\nciteturn9572view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 13; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.10030v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 702\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Pop-Stack Operator on Ornamentation Lattices (https://arxiv.org/html/2501.10311v1)\nciteturn9572view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.10311v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 456\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPop-Stack Operators for Torsion Classes and Cambrian Lattices (https://arxiv.org/html/2312.03959v1)\nciteturn9572view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn9570view2\",\"pattern\":\"Theorem 5.3\"}); Total lines: 1931\nL548: ### 5.1. Pop-stack and mutation\nL549: \nL550: We now prove our first main result, which describes the relationship between the pop-stack operators and the mutation of 2-term simple-minded collections.\nL551: \nL552: ###### Theorem 5.1.\nL553: \nL554: For $(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})\\in\\smc\\Lambda$, let $\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y}):=\\mu_{\\mathcal{X}}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})$ and $\\mu_{\\uparrow}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y}):=\\mu_{\\mathcal{Y}}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})$. There are commutative diagrams as follows:\nL555: \nL556:  |  |\nL569: One can also generalize the proof of cite86†Theorem 5.1 to show that the torsion classes of the form $\\mathsf{T}(\\mu_{\\mathcal{X}^{\\prime}}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y}))$ (with $\\mathcal{X}^{\\prime}\\subseteq\\mathcal{X}$) are precisely those obtained by intersecting $\\mathsf{T}(\\mathcal{X})$ with a subset of the torsion classes it covers.\nL574: ###### Theorem 5.3.\nL575: \nL576: Let $\\mathcal{T},\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$. Then $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})=\\mathcal{T}$ if and only if both of the following hold.\nL577: \nL578:   1. (1)\nL579: \nL580: There is an inclusion $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})$.\nL581: \nL582:   2. (2)\nL583: \nL584: For every $X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$, there exists $Z\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))$ admitting a surjection $Z\\twoheadrightarrow X$.\nL604: by cite86†Theorem 5.1 , so in particular, cite129†1 holds. It follows from cite96†Theorem 4.6 cite130†2 that\nL605: \nL606:  | $${\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))=(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))}.$$  |\nL607: \nL608: Finally, because $\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))=(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))$, cite131†2 must hold by cite120†Definition 4.4 .\nL610:  | $$\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))=\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))=(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})).$$  |\nL611: \nL612: We conclude that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})=\\mathcal{T}$ by cite86†Theorem 5.1 . ∎\nL613: \nL614: We now characterize the 1-pop-stack sortable elements of $\\tors\\Lambda$.\nL615: ###### Corollary 5.4.\nL616: \nL617: Let $\\mathcal{T}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$. The following are equivalent.\nL618: \nL619:   1. (1)\nL620: \nL621: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\hat{0}$.\nL622: \nL623:   2. (2)\nL624: \nL625: Every brick in $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$ is simple.\nL626: \nL627:   3. (3)\nL628: \nL629: The torsion class $\\mathcal{T}$ is a Serre subcategory of $\\mods\\Lambda$.\nL630: \nL631:   4. (4)\nL632: \nL633: There exists a nonzero injective module $I$ such that $\\mathcal{T}=\\prescript{\\perp}{}{I}$.\nL634: \nL635: Dually, the following are equivalent.\nL636: \nL637:   1. (1)\nL638: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\mods\\Lambda$.\nL639: \nL640:   2. (2)\nL641: \nL642: Every brick in $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})$ is simple.\nL643: \nL644:   3. (3)\nL645: \nL646: The torsion-free class $\\mathcal{T}^{\\perp}$ is a Serre subcategory of $\\mods\\Lambda$.\nL647: \nL648:   4. (4)\nL649: \nL650: There exists a nonzero projective module $P$ such that $\\mathcal{T}=\\Gen(P)$.\nL651: ###### Proof.\nL652: \nL653: We prove only the first result as the second is dual. The equivalences among cite132†2 , cite133†3 , and cite134†4 are contained in cite135†Proposition 3.10 .\nL654: \nL655: To see that cite136†1 implies cite132†2 , suppose $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\hat{0}$. Then every brick in $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))$ is simple. But $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))$ by cite137†Theorem 5.3 .\nL656: We now show that cite133†3 implies cite136†1 . Suppose $\\mathcal{T}$ is a Serre subcategory. Then in particular, $\\mathcal{T}$ is closed under submodules. Thus, for $Y\\in\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})$, the map $g_{Y,\\mathcal{X}}$ must be surjective. cite86†Theorem 5.1 then implies that $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))=\\emptyset$, so $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=0$. ∎\nL657: The following characterizes all 2-pop-stack sortable elements of $\\tors\\Lambda$; it also describes the image under $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$ of each 2-pop-stack sortable element.\nL658: ###### Corollary 5.5.\nL659: \nL660: Let $\\mathcal{T}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$, and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be a set of simple modules. Then $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$ if and only if the following all hold.\nL661: \nL662:   1. (1)\nL663: \nL664: We have $\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))=0=\\mathrm{Ext}^{1}_{\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))$.\nL665: \nL666:   2. (2)\nL667: The socle^{2}^{2} 2 Recall that the socle $\\mathrm{soc}M$ of a module $M$ is the sum of all of its semisimple submodules. $\\mathrm{soc}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))$ of $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$ satisfies $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})/\\mathrm{soc}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$.\nL668: \nL669:   3. (3)\nL670: \nL671: There does not exist $S\\in\\mathcal{S}$ with $\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),S)=0$. (Equivalently, there is a surjection $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\twoheadrightarrow\\mathcal{S}$.)\nL672: ###### Proof.\nL673: Suppose first that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$. Since $\\mathcal{D}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))=\\mathcal{S}$, it follows from cite137†Theorem 5.3 that $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))$. Then the fact that cite138†1 holds follows from the definition of a 2-term simple-minded collection.\nL674: cite137†Theorem 5.3 also implies that for every $S\\in\\mathcal{S}$, there exists $Z\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))$ that admits a surjection $Z\\twoheadrightarrow S$. This shows that cite139†3 holds. To prove cite140†2 , consider ${X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))}$. Since $\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$ is closed under submodules, the map $g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}\\colon X\\rightarrow S_{X}$ must be surjective.\nL675: Moreover, we have that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))=0$ by cite87†Corollary 5.4 . cite86†Theorem 5.1 implies that $\\mathrm{ker}g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}$ is in $\\mathcal{U}(0)$, which is precisely the set of simple modules. If follows that $\\mathrm{ker}g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}\\subseteq\\mathrm{soc}(X)$ and therefore that $X/\\mathrm{soc}(X)\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$.\nL676: To prove the converse, suppose $\\mathcal{T}$ satisfies cite138†Items 1 , cite140†2 and cite139†3 . Then cite138†1 implies that $(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))$ is a semibrick pair. Moreover, for $X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$, the map $g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}$ must be surjective since $\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$ is closed under submodules. This map must also have a semisimple kernel by cite140†2 .\nL677: We then have that $\\mu_{\\mathcal{S}}(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}))=(\\emptyset,\\mathcal{S}\\cup\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime})$ for $\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime}=\\{\\mathrm{ker}g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}\\mid X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\}$. cite141†Proposition A.3 therefore implies that $\\mathrm{ker}g_{X,\\mathcal{S}}$ is simple for all $X$.\nL678: Letting $\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime\\prime}$ denote the set of all simple modules, we find that $(\\emptyset,\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime\\prime})$ is a 2-term simple-minded collection satisfying $\\mathcal{S}\\cup\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime}\\subseteq\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime\\prime}$. By cite142†Proposition A.4 , $(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{Y}):=\\mu_{\\mathcal{S}}(\\emptyset,\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime\\prime})$ is a 2-term simple-minded collection satisfying $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{Y}$.\nL679: Now note that $\\mathcal{Y}=\\mathcal{U}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))$ by cite112†Theorem 3.7 . Hence, $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\Filt(\\mathcal{S}))$. Together with cite139†3 , this allows us to use cite137†Theorem 5.3 to conclude that $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\Filt(\\mathcal{S})$. ∎\nL680: ### 5.3. The image of pop-stack\nL681: \nL682: We now build toward a complete description of the images of $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$ and $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$ (and, hence, the facets of the canonical join complex of $\\tors\\Lambda$) for any $\\tau$-tilting finite algebra $\\Lambda$. Our first result is proven more generally for arbitrary semidistrim lattices in [cite59†DW23 , Section 9]. We give a new representation-theoretic proof for lattices of torsion classes.\nL683: ###### Proposition 5.6.\nL684: \nL685: Let $\\mathcal{T}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$.\nL686: \nL687:   1. (1)\nL688: \nL689: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))\\subseteq\\mathcal{T}$.\nL690: \nL691:   2. (2)\nL692: \nL693: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))\\supseteq\\mathcal{T}$.\nL694: \nL695:   3. (3)\nL696: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})))=\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})$.\nL697: \nL698:   4. (4)\nL699: \nL700: We have $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})))=\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})$.\nL701: ###### Proof.\nL702: \nL703: We prove only cite143†1 and cite144†3 as the proofs of cite145†2 and cite146†4 are similar.\nL704: \nL705: We have $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{D}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})))$ by cite86†Theorem 5.1 . By cite110†Theorem 3.4 , this implies cite143†1 .\nL706: To prove cite144†3 , let $(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})=(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))$. Let $(\\mathcal{X}_{1},\\mathcal{Y}_{1})=\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{X},\\mathcal{Y})$ and $(\\mathcal{X}_{2},\\mathcal{Y}_{2})=\\mu_{\\uparrow}(\\mathcal{X}_{1},\\mathcal{Y}_{1})$. By cite86†Theorem 5.1 , we need to show that $\\mu_{\\downarrow}(\\mathcal{X}_{2},\\mathcal{Y}_{2})=(\\mathcal{X}_{1},\\mathcal{Y}_{1})$.\nL707: By cite120†Definition 4.4 , this is equivalent to showing that $\\mathcal{X}_{2}=\\mathcal{Y}_{1}$, which is equivalent to showing that $g_{\\mathcal{Y}_{1},Z}$ is surjective for every $Z\\in\\mathcal{X}_{1}$.\nL827: Suppose that cite155†1 holds and that $\\mathcal{T}$ is not in the image of $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}$. By cite149†Corollary 5.7 , this means there exists $X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$ such that $g_{\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}),X}$ is injective. We will prove that $X$ is projective by induction on $|\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})|$. First suppose $|\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})|=1$. Then cite86†Theorem 5.1 implies that ${|\\mathcal{D}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))|=|\\Lambda|}$.\nL828: Now, $|\\mathcal{D}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))|+|\\mathcal{U}(\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}))|=|\\Lambda|$ by cite112†Theorem 3.7 and [cite81†KY14 , Corollary 5.5]^{3}^{3} 3 This is a classical and well-known fact that was proved much earlier than [cite81†KY14 ], but this logical deduction suits the organization of this paper., so $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\mods\\Lambda$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPop-Stack Operators for Torsion Classes and Cambrian Lattices (https://arxiv.org/html/2312.03959v1)\nciteturn9572view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn9570view2\",\"pattern\":\"9 Cambrian\"}); Total lines: 1931\nNo matching tex